def article(slug,title,desc,content):
    if slug.startswith('guides/'):
        chunks=re.split(r'(?=<h2)',content)
        content='<div class="reading-grid">'+''.join(f'<section class="learning-box">{part}</section>' for part in chunks if part.strip())+'</div><p><a href="/guides/">Explore all 21 study guides →</a></p>'
    shell(slug,f'{title} | GradeTargetCalc',desc,f'<div class="wrap"><header class="page-hero"><div class="crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / {link("guides","Guides") if slug.startswith("guides/") else "GradeTargetCalc"}</div><h1>{title}</h1><p class="lead">{desc}</p></header><article class="content">{content}</article></div>')
article('guides/weighted-vs-points-grades','Weighted grades vs. total points','Choose the calculation that matches the way your course awards marks.',f'''<h2>Start with the syllabus</h2><p>A course can use assessment categories or a pool of points. These are different weighting systems. Before combining marks, find out which one your instructor uses.</p><h2>A points-based course</h2><p>Suppose you earn 18/20 on a quiz and 70/100 on an exam. Pooling the points gives 88/120 = 73.33%. The exam matters more because more points were available. Use the {link('grade-percentage-calculator','percentage calculator')} with the combined points.</p><h2>A category-weighted course</h2><p>Now suppose quizzes are worth 40% and the exam is worth 60%. The quiz percentage is 90%, so the course grade is 90 × 0.40 + 70 × 0.60 = 78%. The same marks give a different result because the syllabus assigns different importance. Use the {link('weighted-grade-calculator','weighted grade calculator')}.</p><h2>Why averaging percentages can go wrong</h2><p>The simple average of 90% and 70% is 80%. That is correct only if both assessments have equal weight. It is neither the points-based result nor the 40/60 category result above.</p><h2>Incomplete work is not always a zero</h2><p>If only the 90% quiz category has been completed, your normalized current average is 90%. Its contribution to the full course is 36 percentage points. Treating the ungraded exam as zero would produce 36%, which answers a different question. Check whether your online gradebook includes ungraded assessments before using its current percentage.</p><h2>Categories with multiple assignments</h2><p>Calculate each category’s own average first, using the instructor’s rules. Then apply category weights to those category averages. Do not apply the entire category weight separately to each assignment. Dropped scores and extra credit can change the category average.</p><p>Next: {link('final-grade-calculator','work out the final exam score you need')}.</p>''')
article('guides/what-grade-do-i-need-to-pass','What grade do I need to pass?','Use your real course threshold and exam weight to set a realistic target.',f'''<h2>Find the actual pass requirements</h2><p>Check your syllabus for the passing percentage, final exam weight and any separate requirement to pass the final itself. A calculator can solve a weighted-average target; it cannot waive a mandatory exam score or attendance rule.</p><h2>Work backward from the target</h2><p>Imagine a course where your current grade, excluding the final, is 62%. You need a 60% course grade and the final counts for 40%. Your completed work contributes 62 × 0.60 = 37.2 percentage points. The final must supply 60 − 37.2 = 22.8 points. Divide by 0.40 to get 57% on the final.</p><p>Enter these values in the {link('final-grade-calculator','final grade calculator')}. Compare targets only after checking the inputs: a gradebook that counts ungraded work as zero can make your current percentage unsuitable.</p><h2>Convert a percentage to test questions carefully</h2><p>On an equally weighted 50-question exam, 57% corresponds to 28.5 correct answers. You cannot answer half a whole question correctly in this model, so you need at least 29 correct, or 58%. This changes when partial credit or different question weights apply. The {link('test-score-calculator','test score calculator')} shows raw percentages.</p><h2>Leave room for uncertainty</h2><p>A mathematical minimum is a planning reference. Course rounding rules, a change in assessment weights or an incorrectly entered current grade can change the outcome. Plan for a score above the minimum and ask your instructor about ambiguous rules.</p><h2>If the target is out of reach</h2><p>A required score above 100% is not attainable on a standard exam capped at 100%. Recheck the weights, then ask about legitimate options such as reassessment or academic support. A negative required score means the weighted average already meets the target even with zero on the final, but separate course requirements still apply.</p>''')
article('guides/gpa-scales-us-uk-canada','GPA scales in the US, UK & Canada','Why the same percentage does not always mean the same GPA.',f'''<h2>The formula is portable; grading rules are not</h2><p>A credit-weighted GPA divides total quality points by GPA-bearing credits. The harder question is which points and credits belong in that calculation. Use an institution’s published rules rather than a generic conversion chart.</p><h2>United States: check points and rounding</h2><p>The University of Illinois explains quality points as grade points multiplied by course credit hours. Its official GPA is truncated to two decimal places. Our planning calculators round to two decimals, so even a correct input can differ slightly from an official transcript.</p><p><a href="https://www.registrar.illinois.edu/courses-grades/calculate-your-gpa/">Source: University of Illinois GPA method</a></p><h2>United Kingdom: a weighted mark is not a degree award</h2><p>UK module marks may be weighted by credits, and degree results may also weight years differently. Manchester describes degree classes using weighted marks and publishes separate regulations. A general module average cannot apply every classification or borderline rule.</p><p><a href="https://www.manchester.ac.uk/study/international/study-abroad-exchange/support/grading-assessment/">Source: Manchester grading and assessment</a></p><h2>Canada: use the evaluating institution’s scale</h2><p>UBC publishes percentage grades and explains that a GPA equivalent requires the evaluating body’s scale. Multiplying a percentage by 4/100 is not a universal conversion. For applications, first check whether the recipient wants the original mark or its own conversion.</p><p><a href="https://students.ok.ubc.ca/academic-success/grades-performance/">Source: UBC grades and performance</a></p><h2>What to check before calculating</h2><ul><li>The grade points assigned to each grade.</li><li>Which courses count toward GPA, including transfer, pass/fail and withdrawn courses.</li><li>Whether repeated courses replace previous attempts.</li><li>Whether credits use semester, quarter or another unit system.</li><li>Whether the institution rounds or truncates results.</li></ul><p>Use the {link('gpa-calculator','GPA and semester calculator')} with institution-defined numeric points, or the {link('weighted-grade-calculator','weighted calculator')} for percentage module averages.</p>''')
